<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.4"/>
    <code code="10164-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="History of Present illness Narrative"/>
    <title>History of Present Illness</title>
    <text><paragraph>This fictional modern episode addresses the presenting concerns below. [S]</paragraph><list><item>Moving her breast cancer survivorship care to primary care, with the oncology team kept informed; mild hot flashes on tamoxifen. [S]</item><item>Wants complete information and to make her own decisions. She chose a flat closure without reconstruction and does not wish to wear an external prosthesis; she asks that this not be raised again unless she raises it. [S]</item></list><paragraph>Historical basis. These claims are historical evidence, not findings from a modern examination.</paragraph><list><item>Audre Lorde was born Audrey Geraldine Lorde on February 18, 1934, in New York City; her parents were immigrants from Grenada. [D]</item><item>Lorde was a poet, essayist, and novelist. In her poetry and prose she wrote about matters important to her as a woman of color, as a lesbian, as a mother, and as a feminist; a reference biography describes her as an African American lesbian feminist and poet. [D]</item><item>In 1977 a doctor found a tumor in Lorde&apos;s breast that, after about three weeks of uncertainty, proved benign. In a talk given that year to the Modern Language Association she said: &quot;Your silence will not protect you.&quot; [D]</item><item>On Labor Day 1978, during a routine self-examination, Lorde found a lump in her right breast; a diagnosis of malignancy and a mastectomy followed. She described the experience in The Cancer Journals. [D]</item><item>After her mastectomy Lorde chose never to hide the fact of her missing breast. She refused to wear the &quot;wad of lambswool pressed into a pale pink breast-shaped pad&quot; pushed on her by nurses and medical counselors, and she was repeatedly scolded for posing a threat to cancer patient &quot;morale.&quot; [D]</item></list><paragraph>4 later historical claims are omitted because they describe events after the index age. See the teaching note.</paragraph></text>

  </section>
</component>

<component>
  <section>
